// Surface fold-in — user-written typed holes `?` and `?name`.
//
// GRADUATION: user holes are FIRST-CLASS surface, not incomplete input. A `?`
// (anonymous) or `?name` (named, addressing the hole in the goal stream) is a
// legitimate axiom — the melder molds it to a complete hole meld with ZERO
// obligations, and its optional name attaches as the `hole_name` tail. This
// fixture is the parser-side witness (wyrd-bsmf) that a hole never absorbs the
// tile closing an enclosing form: the `;` / `}` / `,` / `)` after a hole, and
// an infix operator beside one, all stay flat siblings. The hole SEMANTICS —
// each hole reported as a typed GOAL, holey items left unevaluated — are the
// typing machine's (model example 12-typed-holes covers the `{ }` block hole);
// the consumer of this structural guarantee is the lower.rs migration
// (wyrd-ohzn), which needs the closer to remain a flat sibling for block
// recovery. Parse-only, like the rest of the surface tree.

// A bare anonymous hole as a def value: the `;` terminator stays a sibling.
def anon = ?;

// A named hole: the name attaches to the hole, the `;` stays a sibling.
def named = ?goal;

// Holes at operand positions in an infix chain (both bare and named).
def in_infix = ? + 1;
def named_infix = ?lhs + ?rhs;

// Holes as call arguments: the `,` and `)` stay flat siblings.
def in_call = f(?, 2);

// Holes inside a thunk block (computation position): the `}` closes the block,
// not the hole.
def in_thunk = thunk { ? };
def seeded = thunk { ret ?seed };

// A hole in each tuple slot: the `,` and `)` stay siblings.
def pair_hole = (?, ?slot);
